  "narrative": "I interpreted all newly delivered estimates under the ledger sign convention. For direction -1 comparisons, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a more negative CRISPR dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency. For the direction +1 NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss comparisons, a positive estimate means weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Every due claim was a pre-specified robustness/context probe rather than a new discovery test, and all comparison cells satisfied the minimum-cell rule; the smallest exposed cell was H165 with 27 exposed lines, which is still above the required 20 but warrants more caution than the larger strata.\n\nThe high screen-quality stratum screen_roc_auc >= 0.96 is informative because it complements the previously accepted low-quality stratum and reduces the concern that the core associations are artifacts of poor screen discrimination. All four core associations persisted with large effects and intervals excluding zero: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency +0.803 [0.774, 0.832] in H156; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency +0.784 [0.745, 0.823] in H157; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency +0.796 [0.765, 0.827] in H158; and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency +0.230 [0.153, 0.308] in H159. These lower bounds are far above zero and far above a 10% relative/clinically meaningful benchmark on these dependency scales. I accept H156-H159 as dataset-specific robustness evidence that the core associations are not driven only by lower-quality screens.\n\nThe adherent-culture probes also supported robustness for two core effects not previously confirmed in that exact culture context. H160 SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency was +0.788 [0.765, 0.811], and H161 KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency was +0.792 [0.768, 0.815]. These are large, precise, and clinically large relative to the outcome scales. I accept H160 and H161 and close them. This supports the interpretation that the associations are not simply artifacts of a non-adherent or mixed-culture subset.\n\nThe high mutation-burden stratum mutation_burden >= 8 tested whether the core associations remain in genomic contexts that might be enriched for co-occurring mutational processes or MSI-related biology. H162 SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency was +0.824 [0.787, 0.860], and H163 PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency was +0.276 [0.181, 0.371]. Both intervals exclude zero and the effects remain very large relative to the 10% benchmark. I accept H162 and H163. This reduces concern that the SMARCA2 and STAT3 associations are driven mainly by low-mutation-burden contexts.\n\nThe high-aneuploidy probes aneuploidy_score >= 0.5 tested contexts where copy-number instability and chromosome-level imbalance could distort dependency scores or increase screen noise. H164 SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency was +0.755 [0.714, 0.795]; H165 MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency was +0.824 [0.756, 0.891]; H166 KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency was +0.797 [0.752, 0.842]; and H167 PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency was +0.219 [0.114, 0.323]. Although H165 and H167 have smaller exposed cells than the other strata, their lower bounds remain well above zero and well above the clinically meaningful scale. I accept H164-H167, with the caveat that these are smaller subgroup estimates and therefore less precise than the main strata. They still provide strong evidence that the core effects persist in highly aneuploid lines.\n\nMultiplicity requires consideration. These were 12 pre-specified confirmatory probes, and under a global null one would expect fewer than one false positive on average at alpha 0.05. All 12 are positive, all intervals exclude zero, and most lower bounds are extremely far from zero. Even with Bonferroni-style thinking at about alpha 0.004, the qualitative conclusions would not change because the lower bounds are not marginal. However, these are still internal dataset analyses and many contexts overlap in the same lines, so they are not independent external replication. They should be described as strong dataset-specific association evidence and robustness evidence, not as proof of causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or generalizability.\n\nNo new validation request is made because the remaining new voluntary validation-request capacity is zero and the current claims already have extensive internal robustness. The main scientific conclusion remains that four associations are highly supported within this supplied lung-lineage dataset: SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and PTEN loss with much stronger STAT3 dependency. These now survive pre-specified high screen quality, adherent culture, high mutation burden, and high aneuploidy contexts. The most informative next evidence would be external replication or adjusted multivariable modeling controlling for library, batch, culture, screen quality, ploidy, mutation burden, lineage subtype, and co-occurring alterations. Further exploratory interaction hunting in this dataset would have low marginal value unless externally motivated and pre-specified.",
  "research_notes": "Updated after iteration 12 appraise. H156-H167 were all newly delivered high-priority robustness probes and are now accepted and closed. The four core dataset-specific associations now have robustness support across high screen_roc_auc >=0.96, adherent culture, mutation_burden >=8, and aneuploidy_score >=0.5 contexts, in addition to prior accepted low-screen-quality, batch, KRAS/TP53/PIK3CA/STK11/ARID1A, lineage adenocarcinoma, library, and other contexts. The supported associations remain: SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss with much stronger STAT3 dependency. All accepted effects far exceed the approximately 10% clinical-meaningfulness benchmark and are not marginal under multiplicity considerations. Caveats: all evidence remains from the supplied dataset; subgroup strata overlap and are not independent external validation; associations are not causal or generalizable without replication. New voluntary validation requests are exhausted. Further exploratory internal interaction discovery should stop unless externally motivated and pre-specified. The most valuable next work would be external replication and adjusted multivariable analysis controlling CRISPR library, screen batch, culture type, screen quality, ploidy, mutation burden, lineage subtype, and co-occurring mutations."
