aker dependency. Discovery R8/R13 was +0.796 with interval +0.776 to +0.816, and validation R66 was +0.791 with interval +0.763 to +0.820. Fourth, PTEN loss is associated with much stronger STAT3 dependency. The main effect was discovery R25 +0.267 with interval +0.215 to +0.319 and validation R26 +0.268 with interval +0.188 to +0.348. The KY-library-specific claim H149 now has discovery R137 +0.158 and validation R145 +0.317, with the validation lower bound exceeding the approximate 0.10 benchmark.\n\nThese four findings are supported by both effect size and consistency. In the main claims, point estimates and independent validation intervals are far larger than zero and far above the approximate 0.10 clinical benchmark used for interpretation. They also persist across many internally specified strata, including complete matched RNA/DNA/CRISPR QC, screen-quality bands, CRISPR libraries including Avana and KY, screen batch, adherent culture, lineage subtype, mutation-burden strata, high and lower-median aneuploidy, and several co-occurring alteration contexts such as TP53, KRAS, PIK3CA, STK11, and ARID1A contexts. This makes the findings internally robust, although the strata overlap and remain part of the same supplied dataset.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings are also important. EGFR amplification was not supported as a meaningful predictor of EGFR dependency after validation. ERBB2 amplification did not show supported association with ERBB2 dependency. KRAS mutation, BRAF V600E, TP53 loss effects on MDM2 or RPL11, MYC amplification effects across several outcomes, RB1 loss effects on POLR2A or KIF18A, BRCA2 loss, STK11 loss, SMAD4 loss, PIK3CA mutation as a direct predictor, NF1 loss, ARID1A loss alone, and CDKN2A loss independent of SMARCA4 status were either rejected as null in available tests or remain unresolved without sufficient evidence for clinically meaningful effects. The constructed research markers D, E, and F behaved as expected negative controls,