  "narrative": "No new validation response deadlines were delivered and no voluntary validation requests remain, so this final synthesis does not change conclusions on the basis of newly released independent evidence. The scientific record is therefore based on unadjusted discovery comparisons in the supplied 2000-line lung-lineage CRISPR dataset plus already completed independent validations. All conclusions are internal associations only; they do not establish causal mechanisms, therapeutic vulnerability, or generalizability beyond this dataset. Because dependency scores are signed and often centered below zero, relative percent changes near zero are unstable, so the analysis has continued to use a conservative absolute signed dependency-score benchmark of about 0.10 in the registered direction. In the signed reporting convention used here, a positive estimate for direction -1 means the exposed or interaction subgroup has more negative, i.e. stronger, dependency; for direction +1, a positive estimate means the exposed or interaction subgroup has less negative, i.e. weaker, dependency, when the exposure contrast is defined that way.   SUPPORTED ROBUST FINDINGS. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Core evidence H6/R6 is estimate +0.784, 95% interval +0.765 to +0.803; independent validation R53 is +0.804, interval +0.775 to +0.833. This effect is enormous relative to the SMARCA2 dependency standard deviation and is reproduced across many internal eligibility strata, including lineage subtype, library, screen-quality, ploidy, mutation burden, Cas9 activity, culture, age, and co-mutation-negative contexts. This is the strongest internal synthetic-lethality signal in the run.   Second, MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency. Core evidence H7/R7 is estimate +0.790, interval +0.760 to +0.819; independent validation R40 is +0.812, interval +0.765 to +0.858. This association is also broadly reproduced, including in TP53-loss context H83/R60 +0.833 interval +0.765 to +0.900, validated R79 +0.760 interval +0.674 to +0.847.   Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, which is the reverse of the original matched-genotype hypothesis. Original direction H8/R8 is rejected; the reverse claim H13/R8 is accepted with estimate +0.796, interval +0.776 to +0.816, and independent validation R66 +0.791 interval +0.763 to +0.820. This is a large and reproducible internal association, but its biological interpretation requires caution because it contradicts the initial expectation.   Fourth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. Core evidence H41/R25 is estimate +0.267, interval +0.215 to +0.319; independent validation R26 is +0.268 interval +0.188 to +0.348. This effect is smaller than the genotype-dependency core effects but is clinically meaningful under the 0.10 benchmark and is broadly reproduced across many eligibility strata. The direct EMT-stratum tests are decisive: EMT >= 1.0 H290/R284 estimate +0.411 interval +0.219 to +0.603 and EMT <= 0.999 H291/R285 estimate +0.253 interval +0.199 to +0.307. The EMT interaction itself, including the more extreme EMT >= 1.0 test H288/R283 estimate +0.158 interval -0.040 to +0.357, is not decisive for effect modification. The simplest supported interpretation is a broadly present PTEN-loss/STAT3 association without demonstrated EMT-specific modification.   Fifth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. This was accepted from the discovery evidence H24/R293 and the equivalent reverse-coded claim H216/R293: estimate +0.802, interval +0.782 to +0.822. It has not received independent validation because the validation budget is exhausted, so it should be viewed as a strong internal association requiring external replication before biological or therapeutic interpretation.   A separate accepted but less certain subgroup finding is STK11 loss versus STAT3 dependency in age >= 65, H253/R241 estimate +0.258 interval +0.133 to +0.382. Because the overall STK11-loss/STAT3 association H127/R111 was null, +0.015 interval -0.014 to +0.043, and because this subgroup finding is not independent validated, it is best treated as hypothesis-generating rather than a core robust result. Most other accepted claims are stratum-specific replications of the four core genotype-dependency associations or the TP53-loss/USP7 association.   NEGATIVE OR UNSUPPORTED FINDINGS. The run also produced many useful negative results. Matched oncogene or addictions were largely unsupported: KRAS mutation for KRAS dependency H1/R1 estimate +0.014 interval -0.004 to +0.031; BRAF V600E for BRAF dependency H2/R2 near null; EGFR amplification for EGFR dependency H3/R3 near null and independently validated as null R13 estimate +0.002 interval -0.043 to +0.046; ERBB2 amplification for ERBB2 dependency H4/R4 null; TP53 loss for MDM2 dependency H5/R5 null; MYC amplification hypotheses for DHX9, POLR2A, MDM2, STAT3, KRAS, WRN, KIF18A, RPL11, and USP7 were generally null or directionally small. RB1 loss for POLR2A dependency was null and independently validated H39/R23 and R39. ARID1A loss for SMARCA2 dependency was null and independently validated H21/R15 and R80. In this final synthesis I also converted two small unresolved mean differences to reject: APC mutation versus TMED10 dependency H96/R75 estimate +0.004 interval -0.020 to +0.027, and BRAF V600E versus RIT1 dependency H98/R77 estimate +0.004 interval -0.025 to +0.033. Both intervals are far below the 0.10 practical benchmark in the registered direction.   Several effect-modification or interaction claims are unsupported or unresolved. The EMT modification of PTEN-loss/STAT3 was not established despite both direct EMT strata showing meaningful PTEN associations. High mutation burden interaction for PTEN/STAT3 H292/R286 is near zero, +0.012 interval -0.101 to +0.125. High-ploidy attenuation of PTEN/STAT3 H285/R263 remains unresolved, estimate +0.033 interval -0.082 to +0.149, because the interval still allows a meaningful interaction even though the point estimate is small. Other wide unresolved interactions include media-serum, sex, age, growth-rate, culture, and some genotype-context interactions; invalid small-cell tests remain noninformative rather than negative. Rejection in this task means the supplied unadjusted analysis does not support the registered clinically meaningful direction or interaction; it does not prove absence in other datasets or adjusted models.   UNRESOLVED UNCERTAINTY. Residual uncertainty is concentrated in: (1) interaction tests whose intervals still allow effects at or above the 0.10 benchmark, especially PTEN-loss/STAT3 modification by ploidy and other environmental or molecular contexts; (2) the unvalidated internal TP53-loss/USP7 association; (3) the STK11-loss/STAT3 age-stratum finding; and (4) invalid small-cell subgroup tests such as low-mutation-burden MSI strata, PTEN/MSI interactions, and Humagne CD KEAP1/NFE2L2 eligibility. These unresolved items are not sufficient to change the accepted core conclusions, but they do define the most informative next analyses.   MOST INFORMATIVE NEXT ANALYSIS. The best next step is not more exploratory unadjusted mean-difference testing within this same dataset, because validation is exhausted and the major mean-difference signal has already been resolved. The most informative follow-up would be an independent external validation or a pre-specified adjusted analysis of the core associations, especially PTEN loss versus STAT3 dependency, TP53 loss versus USP7 dependency, and the SMARCA4/SMARCA2 and MSI/WRN associations. If an interaction question must be prioritized within this dataset, the best candidate is a model-based or carefully powered analysis of EMT status modifying PTEN-loss/STAT3 dependency, because the direct stratum means are strong but the interaction interval remains wide. Overall, the scientifically defensible final conclusions are five internal associations: SMARCA4 loss with SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; with the last of these not independently validated. The negative findings are substantial and should be retained as evidence that many simple matched-genotype dependency expectations are not supported in this dataset.",
  "research_notes": "Final synthesis after validation budget exhaustion. No assessments were due; only final cleanup assessments made: H96 and H98 changed to reject because valid 95% intervals are far below the absolute 0.10 signed benchmark in the registered direction. Accepted core evidence remains H6/R6 + R53 for SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2; H7/R7 + R40 for MSI-high/WRN; H13/R8 + R66 for KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2; H41/R25 + R26 and many strata for PTEN-loss/STAT3; H24/R293 and H216/R293 for TP53-loss weaker USP7, unvalidated. H253 STK11-loss/STAT3 age >= 65 is accepted but should be treated as hypothesis-generating because overall STK11/STAT3 is null. Negative matched genotype tests and validated nulls remain important. Unresolved items are mostly wide interaction tests, especially high ploidy for PTEN/STAT3 H285, and invalid small-cell tests. Next best science: external independent validation and adjusted/pre-specified interaction analysis, especially PTEN/STAT3 EMT modification and TP53/USP7 replication; further internal unadjusted comparisons are unlikely decisive."
}

